A barely detectable band corresponds to 1 cpm = 1 dpm of \(^{32}P\) in the band.

So I need the specific activity of the \(\gamma\)-\(^{32}P\)-ATP in the reaction mixture after dilution by the unlabeled ATP already present.

First compute how much unlabeled ATP was in the extract:

\[
9~\mu L \times 1~\text{mM}
= 9\times 10^{-6}\,L \times 10^{-3}\,\text{mol/L}
= 9\times 10^{-9}\,\text{mol}
= 9~\text{nmol}
\]

The added radioactive ATP is said to be a negligible amount of ATP, so total ATP is essentially 9 nmol, in 10 \(\mu L\) total volume.

Radioactivity added:

\[
10~\mu Ci
\]

Using

\[
1~\mu Ci = 2.22\times 10^6~\text{dpm}
\]

gives

\[
10~\mu Ci = 2.22\times 10^7~\text{dpm}
\]

Thus the specific activity of ATP phosphate available for transfer is

\[
\frac{2.22\times 10^7~\text{dpm}}{9\times 10^{-9}~\text{mol}}
= 2.47\times 10^{15}~\text{dpm/mol}
\]

Now only \(1~\mu L\) of the 10 \(\mu L\) reaction was loaded, so a band with 1 dpm on the gel corresponds to 10 dpm worth of labeled protein in the whole reaction.

Moles of labeled protein in the whole reaction (assuming 1 phosphate per molecule):

\[
n = \frac{10~\text{dpm}}{2.47\times 10^{15}~\text{dpm/mol}}
= 4.05\times 10^{-15}~\text{mol}
\]

Number of molecules:

\[
4.05\times 10^{-15}\times 6.02\times 10^{23}
\approx 2.4\times 10^9~\text{molecules}
\]

Since the question asks how many molecules are in the band (the 1 \(\mu L\) loaded sample), divide by 10:

\[
2.4\times 10^8~\text{molecules}
\]

Check directly: 1 dpm on the gel, with specific activity \(2.47\times 10^{15}\) dpm/mol, gives

\[
\frac{1}{2.47\times 10^{15}}~\text{mol}
= 4.05\times 10^{-16}~\text{mol}
\]

\[
4.05\times 10^{-16}\times 6.02\times 10^{23}
\approx 2.4\times 10^8
\]

So the barely visible band contains about \(2\times 10^8\) labeled protein molecules.

